encyclopedia :the_three_forms do
  name "The Three Forms"
  summary "The Three Forms is a religion, cosmology, and working model that describes coherent resonance as paths closing into stable figures."
  kind :culture
  subkind :belief
  status :complete
  aka "the Trine", "the Triptych"
  topics :resonance, :cosmology, :religion, :subject_resonance
  prevalence :common
  available_globally
  log "2026-09-23 — The third panel now states that the False Form is a real force acting with will, as its own entry records, while most people hold it as doctrine or bench superstition; trimmed the model's closing verdicts and added the workshop and household uses of the figures."

  prose <<~PROSE
    The Three Forms is older than the band-and-bandwidth model the #{encyclopedia_ref :resonant_tuner, "Tuners"} use for #{encyclopedia_ref :resonance, "resonance"}. Keepers date its oldest teachings to the ring era and sometimes earlier. Its central claim is that a vibration traces a shape and tends toward a closed figure, and the figure it closes into determines what it does.

    Most people meet it as a picture. The triptych shows two figures of lawful order, a gold Tetrahedron and a red Triangulum, with a third panel between them that the diagram itself marks as false. Tuners hang cheap printed copies over their benches, and a master often gives an apprentice a triptych on the first day of work. Scholars argue over the geometry. Devotional readers, the keepers of the #{ref :tessellan_communion, "Tessellan Communion"} chief among them, treat the three panels as a creed. Every honest copy carries the same line underneath: *Three forms, one conflict, one truth.*

    The figures travel well beyond the benches. Builders chalk a gold tetrahedron on a new wall before its ringglass is tuned. Kite crews scratch a small red triangle on an engine housing before a first run. Communion households paint both lawful figures over the door of a new home and leave the space between them bare.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :how_it_works, heading: "The Closing of the Figure"
    A coherent resonance traces a path. The narrower the band and the truer the single wavelength, the more strongly that path returns to where it began and closes. A closed figure braces itself, holds its frequency and does reliable work. An open path bleeds, wanders and fails. To tune well, in this model, is to help a vibration find its figure.

    Two figures dominate everything the system builds, because between them they cover the two things resonance is asked to do: hold and move.

    **The Tetrahedron, the geometry of Form.** Four faces brace inward in the most stable close there is. It is the lawful shape of the **structural** band. When ringglass hardens a wall, the Three Forms says the stone is being coaxed toward the tetrahedral close, toward more form and more law, and toward the quality the old panels label *light*, because a fully closed thing is visible and trustworthy. The Tetrahedron is gold in every drawing. It is the figure of the lattice, and the figure the ring itself was built to hold.

    **The Triangulum, the geometry of Will.** The smallest closed figure drives hardest. It is the lawful shape of the **kinetic** band: heat, motion, force. A #{encyclopedia_ref :kite, "kite"} under thrust, a refinery running hot and a current of unopposed motion are all, in this model, resonance closing into the triangle and leaning forward. The Triangulum is red, the color of the work it does. Keepers say that everything that can be honestly made sits between Form and Will.

    The **signal** band occupies the path between the two lawful figures, where a vibration is still resolving toward a close. The triptych leaves that path open. Keepers teach that a signal carried toward the single-wavelength horizon the #{encyclopedia_ref :elves, "elves"} pursued would resolve Form and Will into one perfectly closed figure that holds and moves without waste. They call it the One Truth. Living keepers preserve descriptions of it, and every drawing of it offered so far has been disputed. Resonance scholars describe the same horizon as perfect coherence, and the Three Forms treats it as a destination.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :the_third_panel, heading: "The Figure That Will Not Close"
    The third panel of every complete triptych is the #{ref :the_false_form, "False Form"}: an impossible rhombus whose edges meet in contradictory orientations, shimmering where the other two are solid. In the teaching, its path returns to its beginning while leaving the figure open.

    Workshop triptychs use the panel to show coherence through a violated close. In common speech the False Form names a lie that wears the shape of truth, or the slow opening of a figure left untended. Communion keepers read it as a warning about order without truth: the two lawful figures can make a wall and an engine and still leave the system hollow, and the One Truth is the complete figure toward which both reach. Some houses of the Communion teach that the False Form exists only as a violated drawing and may be drawn freely. Others teach that a false closure exerts force, and permit the panel drawn but never sounded.

    The panel is accurate. The False Form is real: a non-closing geometry that acts with will. The elves recorded it as the force that broke the orbital ring, called it the Adversary, and kept the doctrine *falsehood is not* in public teaching when they withdrew to prepare against its return. Its traces lie at the edges of the world, at Bloom Zone cordons, in Deep Shear crystal and in #{encyclopedia_ref :duskgrain, "duskgrain"}, where tuned paths return to their start with one edge still open. The people who know what stands behind those traces are the hidden elves, the head of the #{ref :displacement_council, "Displacement Council"}, and a handful of scholars and Communion keepers whose colleagues consider their reading of the third panel eccentric.

    Nearly everyone else holds the False Form as doctrine, as a figure of speech or as bench superstition. Crews erase an open rhombus chalked on a hull, leave the old caption unsounded in a depleted field, and stop to retune glass that starts to *shimmer wrong*. Most of them call these habits luck.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :significance, heading: "Observance"
    Devotion to the Three Forms varies from person to person more than from place to place. Most people give the figures no thought beyond the printed triptych on a workshop wall, and the cosmology reaches them as vocabulary. For the devout it is a religion in every ordinary sense, with houses, study, obligations and argument.

    Devotional practice centers on study circles. Participants trace the lattice figures by hand, recite the shared lines with their disputed variants named, and work through the closing sentence the way a court works through a precedent. Concealment and withdrawal run through the oldest arguments: whether the One Truth withholds itself or remains unreached, and whether a figure left undrawn is protected or abandoned. The #{ref :tessellan_communion, "Tessellan Communion"} keeps the discipline's public form; household observance selects among the same readings and exercises. Devout families hang a triptych over a deathbed with the dying person's hand guided along the gold figure, and circles sing the closing line at weddings.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :significance, heading: "A Question of Will"
    The tradition's oldest argument asks whether the Three Forms are principles or beings. Resonance scholarship reads them as models of how vibrations behave. Older devotional readings give the figures action: the Tetrahedron holds the world, the Triangulum drives it, and the False Form presses against both. Keepers recite both readings in the disputed variants at study. On the third figure the older reading is right, and the few keepers who know it say so in circles that mostly hear it as a matter of interpretation.

    Keepers also use the #{encyclopedia_ref :fae, "fae"} as a bodily example of closure. A fae concentrates into a clear figure and diffuses into an occupied haze when that effort fails. In Three Forms teaching, this is order held by will at the scale of one person.
  PROSE

  cue "Cheap printed triptychs hang over workshop benches, where a crew waiting on a repair reads the panels off the wall: gold marks the structural band and red the kinetic, naming what the machine in front of them does before its owner explains."
  cue "A devotional circle traces lattice figures by hand in silence, then recites its lines with the disputed variants named aloud, the way a court reads both precedents."
  affordance "Ask any working hand what a resonance device is doing and the answer comes back as figures closing rather than bands and " \
    "bandwidth, because the picture is what most people learned first. A #{encyclopedia_ref :resonant_tuner, "Clarisant-trained Tuner"} corrects the framing and then uses it anyway."
  pressure "When someone calls a thing a false form, keepers hear the drawn third panel and speakers usually mean the ordinary word for a lie that holds its shape, so an insult can land as a doctrinal claim."
  pressure "A Communion keeper orders a crew off a glass path that keeps returning with one edge open, and the foreman, paid by the finished section, calls it bench superstition and sends them back in."
  variation "Some Communion houses teach that the False Form has no being of its own and may be freely drawn; others keep the same panel as a live warning and permit it drawn but never sounded."
  variation "Most people carry the cosmology as workshop vocabulary and a cheap triptych on the wall; the devout keep houses, study, obligations and the old arguments about whether the figures choose."
end
